 drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.125}=8q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3.\n$$" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382373 13194139536073 true] [17592186382372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382374 13194139536073 true] [17592186382372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382375 13194139536073 true] [17592186382372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382376 13194139536073 true] [17592186382372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382377 13194139536073 true] [17592186382372 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536073 true] [17592186382372 1041 "selection" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382372 1104 #edb/ref 17592186382376 13194139536073 true] [17592186382372 1110 #uuid "69917647-b198-5f68-b57f-3a2e295faad7" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382373 1034 "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+3q=3" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382373 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536073 true] [17592186382373 1111 #uuid "d8025af6-6f05-5c00-b639-0b57d479831d" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382374 1034 "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=4" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382374 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536073 true] [17592186382374 1111 #uuid "a11a9b1c-dc7c-5725-aea7-2b03328b8e42" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382375 1034 "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+6\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382375 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536073 true] [17592186382375 1111 #uuid "f0a12df2-4d9b-5bf1-aa1c-7c0eda7c1b18" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382376 1034 "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382376 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536073 true] [17592186382376 1111 #uuid "0e3b00a6-891d-59d1-8df1-d5f7a1760ffe" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382377 1034 "\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2q=3" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382377 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536073 true] [17592186382377 1111 #uuid "9b59beaa-65b4-5702-9dad-4f2484025c81" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382378 1017 #edb/ref 17592186382379 13194139536073 true] [17592186382378 1089 "q-345120" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382378 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536073 true] [17592186382378 1100 "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n\n$$\nq(t)=4{e}^{-3t}-2{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{5}{11}.\n$$\n\nFind, in terms of $t$ only, an expression for the **current** $I$ in this circuit, in amperes.\n\n$I(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382378 1113 #uuid "d9b75dc7-e664-55bf-ad97-8cdf63faee64" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382378 1145 "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(4{e}^{-3t}-2{e}^{-6t}+\\frac{5}{11}) \\\\  & =-12{e}^{-3t}+12{e}^{-6t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382379 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382380 13194139536073 true] [17592186382379 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382381 13194139536073 true] [17592186382379 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536073 true] [17592186382379 1041 "field-1" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382379 1104 #edb/ref 17592186382380 13194139536073 true] [17592186382379 1110 #uuid "3e7b6ae8-84db-5e82-9b74-56dbc3d84c65" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382380 1034 "-12e^{-3t}+12e^{-6t}" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382380 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536073 true] [17592186382380 1111 #uuid "e3583c34-e19e-5659-99e0-ab659428c8b9" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382381 1034 "4e^{-3t}-2e^{-6t}" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382381 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536073 true] [17592186382381 1111 #uuid "672b1fc5-849a-5b40-9f44-780bca352280" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382382 1017 #edb/ref 17592186382383 13194139536073 true] [17592186382382 1089 "q-345122" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382382 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536073 true] [17592186382382 1100 "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=24.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." 13194139536073 true] [17592186382382 1113 #uuid "8915f1df-76e2-5206-9997-c56b044c99b7" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382382 1145 "The *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0,\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the steady-state charge, we substitute these values into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=24\n$$\n\nSubstituting the steady-state conditions gives\n\n$$\n0+4(0)+8q=24,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n8q=24\\,⟹\\,q=3.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current $I=\\frac{dq}{dt}$ has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $3$ coulombs." 13194139536073 true] [17592186382383 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382384 13194139536073 true] [17592186382383 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536073 true] [17592186382383 1041 "field-1" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382383 1104 #edb/ref 17592186382384 13194139536073 true] [17592186382383 1110 #uuid "94423fcd-91fa-58b7-a231-00bbd913c6cb" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382384 1034 "3" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382384 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536073 true] [17592186382384 1111 #uuid "39ecd252-125d-53b6-b1da-c6f3d5fbf1c9" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382385 1017 #edb/ref 17592186382386 13194139536073 true] [17592186382385 1089 "q-345127" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382385 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536073 true] [17592186382385 1100 "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $15\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $30\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=6\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." 13194139536073 true] [17592186382385 1113 #uuid "a75841e4-1687-53dc-8746-b00a6bfb5621" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382385 1145 "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/d7145f4b394e7675b87ed851e344756ecb8441ce737f8664fbfd4b0e426ef36f.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=30I=30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/10)}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=6\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=6\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=6\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n6\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+30\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=15.\n$$\n\nThe *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n6(0)+30(0)+10q=15,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n10q=15\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{15}{10}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{2}$ coulombs." 13194139536073 true] [17592186382386 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382387 13194139536073 true] [17592186382386 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382388 13194139536073 true] [17592186382386 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536073 true] [17592186382386 1041 "field-1" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382386 1104 #edb/ref 17592186382388 13194139536073 true] [17592186382386 1110 #uuid "b7d2e104-3591-57b8-b21f-04d8fee6197e" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382387 1034 "15" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382387 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536073 true] [17592186382387 1111 #uuid "a4d310d2-2260-5271-b612-b3e8a700a3c9" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382388 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382388 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536073 true] [17592186382388 1111 #uuid "83fe316d-340f-5059-82a9-d59892396090" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382389 1017 #edb/ref 17592186382390 13194139536073 true] [17592186382389 1089 "q-345130" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382389 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139536073 true] [17592186382389 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/50bb98a563a0ee061a0faa4d4390aaf459a2fdfccb4721d5d7e6ddd3102c98b1.png)\n\nFind the steady-state charge for the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." 13194139536073 true] [17592186382389 1113 #uuid "6b4773ff-8f02-5bdd-8f58-602b05f01a07" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382389 1145 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $6\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $12\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=12\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=12I=12\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+12\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=6.\n$$\n\nThe **steady-state charge** is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response (the part of the solution that depends on the initial conditions) eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n4(0)+12(0)+4q=6,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n4q=6\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{6}{4}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged, the inductor acts as a short circuit, and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{2}$ coulombs." 13194139536073 true] [17592186382390 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382391 13194139536073 true] [17592186382390 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536073 true] [17592186382390 1041 "field-1" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382390 1104 #edb/ref 17592186382391 13194139536073 true] [17592186382390 1110 #uuid "6886257a-b23f-5396-81f8-b946865fe975" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382391 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382391 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536073 true] [17592186382391 1111 #uuid "12b2443e-9d72-56eb-b1bb-d6ef542dedea" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382392 1017 #edb/ref 17592186382393 13194139536073 true] [17592186382392 1089 "q-345132" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382392 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536073 true] [17592186382392 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14075717/assets/7cbcdf7d43a99b9558b1d085d2306c22e1d27134a5b0ea428721ce7bd074b725.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{P}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{Q}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382392 1113 #uuid "61885c5a-2af5-5794-bf2c-8ceb10ff2a25" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382392 1145 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $5\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=5\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=5I=5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)=5\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q+\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+5\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+5(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\ +4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (3\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(-5\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha +5\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ -5\\alpha +3\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{5}{17},$ $\\beta =\\frac{3}{17}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-4t}-\\frac{5}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{3}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-5,$ $Q=3,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-5+3=-2.\n$$" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382393 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382394 13194139536073 true] [17592186382393 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382395 13194139536073 true] [17592186382393 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382396 13194139536073 true] [17592186382393 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382397 13194139536073 true] [17592186382393 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382398 13194139536073 true] [17592186382393 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536073 true] [17592186382393 1041 "selection" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382393 1104 #edb/ref 17592186382398 13194139536073 true] [17592186382393 1110 #uuid "4f6ca543-dcfb-5845-9b98-826034062223" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382394 1034 "-8" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382394 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536073 true] [17592186382394 1111 #uuid "cfb873cf-66f2-5cbc-80b8-9f1121b0d9ba" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382395 1034 "-5" 13194139536073 true] [17592186382395 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 1319413953607